WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.630 --> 00:00:06.460
These words may make you think of a thick

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:09.250
fluid—milked from the fangs of a dangerous

00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:10.250
snake.

00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:12.440
But the latest venom science is turning to

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:15.930
new sources of venom: centipedes, assassin

00:00:15.930 --> 00:00:18.290
bugs, and stealthy cone snails.

00:00:18.290 --> 00:00:20.540
With the rise of tools that let scientists

00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:23.340
explore all the proteins, peptides, and small

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:25.580
molecules that make up venom,

00:00:25.580 --> 00:00:27.640
scientists have been able to deconstruct the

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:30.690
potent toxic cocktails made by the world’s

00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:32.460
tiniest mixologists.

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:34.640
Some of those include rare animals that aren’t

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:37.041
easy to raise in the lab.

00:00:37.041 --> 00:00:39.320
Most venomous animals aren’t so rare.

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:43.500
There are more than 220,000 species that brew
up

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:46.420
their own toxic mixes—from all branches

00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:47.569
of the family tree.

00:00:47.569 --> 00:00:50.800
For a long time, researchers focused

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:53.479
on only a small set of venomous animals: spiders,

00:00:53.479 --> 00:00:56.420
snakes, scorpions, and some cone snails

00:00:56.420 --> 00:00:59.829
— looking for new therapeutics and for clues to how

00:00:59.829 --> 00:01:01.680
venom-victim pairs evolved.

00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:05.400
How does venom change an animal's behavior?

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:07.620
Does the killer juice make you less afraid

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:09.120
of intimidating beasties?

00:01:09.130 --> 00:01:11.520
And, why do some animals, like the assassin

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:14.250
bug, evolve two venoms--one for defending

00:01:14.250 --> 00:01:16.979
itself and one for killing its prey?

00:01:16.979 --> 00:01:18.940
The arms race between predator and resistant

00:01:18.940 --> 00:01:22.240
prey has led to complex venoms that are incredibly

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:25.240
precise in their targeting, fast-acting, and

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:27.689
potent--all useful characteristics for medicines.

00:01:29.689 --> 00:01:30.680
The toxins in venoms tend to either attack

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:34.320
the nervous system—paralyzing muscles, lungs,

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:36.640
sometimes blinding their prey

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:40.060
or they disrupt blood-coagulation—causing blood

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:43.090
to coagulate or preventing coagulation

00:01:43.090 --> 00:01:44.740
--either can be deadly.

00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:47.080
Such venoms have helped scientists understand

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:49.700
these systems themselves—a classic example

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:52.380
is using pufferfish venom to find a channel

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:54.539
important to nerve signaling.

00:01:54.539 --> 00:01:56.340
The targeting ability of these venoms

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:58.549
and their strong effects on the body, have also

00:01:58.549 --> 00:02:00.900
led to 6 venom-based drugs,

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:02.200
that have already been approved.

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:04.580
including a drug for diabetes

00:02:04.580 --> 00:02:06.660
from the venom of the gila monster

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:09.300
and a painkiller derived from the venom

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:11.820
of the so-called magical cone snail.

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:13.960
Now, scientists are looking to these new sources

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:16.620
of venom --like the sea anemone-- to treat

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:19.440
autoimmune diseases and the deathstalker scorpion

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:22.800
for an entirely different use-- imaging brain tumors

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:24.000
during surgery.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:26.260
Someday, scientists might just catch up

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:29.160
with these ancient chemists--but don’t count on it.

